`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
The valuable move here wasn't writing code — it was distrusting a stale memory. An earlier engram entry claimed .6 was "closed with full implementation this session," but `br show` said `IN_PROGRESS` and `git status` showed zero uncommitted diffs in the relevant packages. Rather than either blindly re-implementing or blindly trusting the memory, reading the actual `model.go` against every acceptance-criteria line was the only way to resolve the contradiction — and it turned out the work existed, just mislabeled under a different bead's commit message (`9491963`, tagged as `.4`'s phase-2 commit). This "bundled commit" pattern has now shown up across .2, .3, .4, .5, and .6 — a strong signal that this ralph-tui pipeline's commit-per-bead granularity doesn't always match what actually lands per iteration.

For the agent-tui verification, the RFC 5737 black-hole IP (`192.0.2.1`) trick versus an `.invalid` domain is the kind of detail that only surfaces empirically: DNS failure for a reserved non-resolving TLD is fast enough that a snapshot-overwriting swap can land before you finish taking a screenshot, while a routable-but-unreachable IP gives a real multi-second window governed by `gitlab.timeout` — turning a flaky test into a deterministic one.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

